{
  "dom_id": "step-e5299",
  "name": "Example: Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function",
  "problem": "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-x-6}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x .$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)(x-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
